    "body": "`CTxMemPool::GetSortedScoreWithTopology()` gets the global mempool order with a `std::sort` that uses `TxGraph::CompareMainOrder` as comparator. This is N log N comparisons, and it runs with `cs` held. It is used by `getrawmempool`, the P2P `mempool` message, `mempool.dat` dumping and `check()`.\n\n`TxGraph` already keeps all main chunks in an index ordered by `ChunkOrder` (`m_main_chunkindex`), which is the same order as `CompareMainOrder`. This PR adds `TxGraph::GetMainOrder()`, which walks that index and expands each chunk in linearization order. The result is all transactions in `CompareMainOrder` order, in O(N) and with no comparator calls. `GetSortedScoreWithTopology()` now uses it. The old sort is kept in `check()` as an assert that both orders are equal.\n\nThe order is the same, so there is no change in RPC, P2P or `mempool.dat` output.\n\nI ran some preliminar experiments and got:\n\nRyzen 5 3600, x86:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `RpcMempoolTxids` (10k txs) | 5.10 ms | 1.07 ms |\n| `getrawmempool false` handler, 60k txs | 99 ms | 24 ms |\n\nMost of the remaining time in `RpcMempoolTxids` is building the JSON array, not the order. `getrawmempool true` does not change much because `entryToJSON` dominates. The time with `cs` held goes down in the same way, which matters for indexers that call `getrawmempool` every few seconds.\n",
